<h2>Common cosmetic options</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li><a href="/services/teeth-whitening/">Professional teeth whitening</a> — significantly stronger and longer-lasting than over-the-counter strips.</li>
  <li><a href="/services/veneers/">Porcelain veneers</a> — thin, hand-shaped porcelain shells bonded to the front of teeth.</li>
  <li><a href="/services/clear-aligners/">Clear aligners</a> — straighten crooked or shifted teeth without metal braces.</li>
  <li><a href="/services/dental-fillings/">Tooth-colored fillings</a> and bonding — repair small chips, gaps, or worn edges.</li>
  <li><a href="/services/crowns-bridges/">Crown replacements</a> — modern all-ceramic crowns where old metal ones used to be.</li>
  <li>Full smile makeovers — combining several procedures into a single coordinated plan.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Match the treatment to what bothers you</h2>
<p>Most people come to a cosmetic dentist with one specific thing they want changed. Starting from that concern, rather than from a procedure, usually leads to a simpler and more affordable plan.</p>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li><strong>Yellowed or stained teeth.</strong> <a href="/services/teeth-whitening/">Professional whitening</a> is usually the first step. Yellow staining responds best; deep gray or brown staining may need <a href="/services/veneers/">veneers</a> instead.</li>
  <li><strong>A chipped or worn front tooth.</strong> Small chips can often be repaired with tooth-colored bonding in a single visit. Larger breaks may need a veneer or a crown.</li>
  <li><strong>Gaps or crowding.</strong> <a href="/services/clear-aligners/">Clear aligners</a> move the teeth themselves. Veneers can close small gaps or mask mild crowding when you'd rather not wear aligners.</li>
  <li><strong>Old dark fillings or metal crowns.</strong> Replacing them with tooth-colored fillings or all-ceramic <a href="/services/crowns-bridges/">crowns</a> often changes the look of a smile more than people expect.</li>
  <li><strong>Missing teeth.</strong> Gaps from lost teeth are restored with <a href="/services/dental-implants/">dental implants</a> or a bridge, often as part of a larger makeover plan.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How we plan cosmetic work</h2>
<p>Every cosmetic case starts with a real conversation about what bothers you and what you'd like the outcome to look like. We use digital imaging and try-in mockups so you can see and approve the plan before any final work is done. We won't talk you into veneers if a simpler option will get the job done.</p>
<p>Healthy teeth and gums come first. If a cleaning, gum treatment, or cavity repair is needed, we take care of it before cosmetic work so the result holds up and looks even.</p>

<h2>How long does it take?</h2>
<p>Whitening can be done in one visit. Veneers and crowns generally take two visits over a few weeks. Clear aligner cases typically run 6 to 18 months depending on the amount of movement. Full makeovers are usually phased over a few months.</p>

<h2>Cost and financing</h2>
<p>Cosmetic treatment can be planned in phases, so you can start with the change that matters most to you and add more later. We'll go over the cost of each option at your consultation. Most dental plans treat purely cosmetic work as elective, so we offer <a href="/insurance-financing/">Cherry financing and in-house payment plans</a> to spread the cost.</p>

<p>Explore examples in our <a href="/before-after-gallery/">before-and-after gallery</a>. Individual results vary, and your consultation will focus on options appropriate for you.</p>
